PART 17. SEARCH, NAVIGATION AND INTERACTION RULES

Every visible button must have a meaningful action.

Examples:

* Save Opportunity must save the selected opportunity.
* View Details must open the correct opportunity.
* Submit My Work must submit the work to the correct challenge.
* Create Challenge must open the challenge creation flow.
* Send Feedback must save and display the feedback.
* Edit Profile must open the profile editor.
* Upgrade to Premium must open the Premium page.

Do not create decorative buttons that do nothing.

Use consistent navigation and clear back buttons.

Provide:

* Loading states.
* Empty states.
* Success messages.
* Error messages.
* Form validation.
* Confirmation before destructive actions.

Use realistic demo content rather than empty placeholder screens.

PART 18. DATA STRUCTURE AND PERMISSIONS

Design the application around these main data entities:

* Users.
* Student Profiles.
* Teacher Profiles.
* Academic Interests.
* Opportunities.
* Professor Challenges.
* Challenge Submissions.
* Feedback.
* Portfolio Achievements.
* Growth Plans.
* Growth Plan Tasks.
* Saved Opportunities.
* Saved Profiles.
* Discussions.
* Comments.
* Reports.
* Notifications.
* Subscription Status.

Important access rules:

* Students can edit their own profiles and portfolios.
* Students can view their own private submissions and feedback.
* Teachers can manage only their own challenges and associated submissions.
* Teachers can view only student profiles made visible to them.
* Administrators can review reported content according to their permissions.
* Private files must not be accessible through public URLs.
* Role permissions must be enforced on the backend, not only hidden in the interface.

If a backend is not configured, create a clearly labeled interactive demo using mock data. Do not pretend that data is securely stored or shared in production.

