* Discussions and moderation prototype.

Do not sacrifice the core experience to implement secondary features.

PART 21. VISUAL PERSONALITY, INTERACTIVE COMPANION & MULTILINGUAL SUPPORT

21.1. Refined Visual Identity 

Refine the existing visual design to make the platform feel softer, warmer, more expressive, and visually memorable.

The interface should use a gentle aesthetic while maintaining a professional international EdTech startup appearance.

Design direction:

* Soft colors to not irritate eyes.
* Light pastel pink.
* Creamy white.
* Pale sage.
* Muted lavender as a subtle optional accent.
* NOT TOO light neutral backgrounds.

Use soft color transitions, subtle decorative details, delicate borders, gentle shadows, and smooth animations.

The interface should feel calm, welcoming, modern, and pleasant to use for extended periods.

Avoid overly saturated colors, harsh contrasts, childish illustrations, excessive gradients, and distracting animations.

Maintain excellent text readability and accessibility.

The pastel aesthetic must be consistent across the landing page, dashboards, cards, forms, notifications, and interactive elements.

21.2. Interactive Companion — A Small Animated Character

Introduce a distinctive interactive companion that becomes a recognizable part of the platform’s identity.

The companion should be a small, cute, rounded square-shaped character.

Visual concept:

* A soft pastel-colored square with rounded corners.
* cute facial expression.
* Small animated eyes and subtle expressions.
* Smooth, playful movements.
* Matcha green, pastel pink, or another color matching the current interface.
* A simple, memorable design that does not distract from the main content.

The character should occasionally slide out from the side of the screen, display a small speech bubble, and make a short comment.

It should feel like a friendly little companion that notices the student’s progress.

Personality

Give the character a playful, witty, slightly sarcastic, encouraging personality.

It can make funny observations, celebrate small achievements, and occasionally make unexpected but harmless comments.

Examples of messages:

Progress and achievements:

* “Wait, you actually completed all your tasks? Iconic.”
* “Your portfolio is getting suspiciously impressive.”
* “No mercy, only studying!!”
* “Look at you being productive. We love character development.”
* “Your academic journey is starting to look interesting.”

Streaks and consistency:

* “Three-day streak! You’re becoming a regular here.”
* “Your streak is alive. Please don’t abandon me now.”
* “Consistency? In this economy? Impressive.”
* “Another day, another tiny step towards your future.”

Portfolio:

* “Your portfolio called. It wants more achievements.”
* “Not to be dramatic, but your projects deserve attention.”
* “Someone’s building an academic reputation.”

Opportunities:

* “Psst. I found something that might interest you.”
* “New opportunity detected. Your future called.”
* “This one looks interesting. Take a look!”

When the student needs motivation:

* “No pressure. One small task is still progress.”
* “You don’t have to figure out your entire future today.”
* “Even ambitious people need breaks. Just saying.”
* “Slow progress is still progress.”

Random playful comments:

* “I was just standing here. Carry on.”
* “Interesting academic decisions are happening today.”
* “Your future self might thank you for this.”
* “I have absolutely no gossip. Only opportunities.”

Context-aware behavior

The companion should respond to actual events in the application whenever possible.

Examples:

* When a student completes a task, celebrate the completion.
* When a student reaches a new streak milestone, acknowledge it.
* When a student adds an achievement, congratulate them.
* When a new opportunity matches their interests, notify them.
* When a student completes their portfolio, celebrate their progress.
* When a student returns after being inactive, welcome them without guilt or pressure.

IMPORTANT:

* Never invent achievements, streaks, statistics, or completed tasks.
* Only make progress-related comments when supported by actual application data.
* Random comments must not pretend to describe something the student has done.
* Avoid guilt-tripping, pressure, or negative comments when students are inactive.
* Do not interrupt important forms, submissions, or reading activities.
* Do not show notifications too frequently.

Interaction

When the character slides out:

* Display a compact speech bubble.
* Allow the student to dismiss the message.
* Allow clicking the character to open a small companion panel.
* Include a mute or disable option in settings.
* Make animations smooth and short.
* Ensure it works on mobile and desktop.

The companion should be a lightweight interface feature, not a replacement for the AI assistants.

Use a predefined set of contextual messages for the prototype. If real AI-generated messages are implemented, keep them short, relevant, and appropriate.

22.3. Multilingual Interface — Kazakh, Russian & English

The platform must support three complete interface languages:

1. Kazakh (Қазақша).
2. Russian (Русский).
3. English.

Add a clearly visible language selector in the main navigation and account settings.

Display language options in their native names:

* Қазақша
* Русский
* English

Language switching behavior

When a user changes the language:

* Update the entire interface immediately.
* Translate navigation labels.
* Translate buttons and forms.
* Translate dashboard sections.
* Translate notifications.
* Translate validation messages and error states.
* Translate onboarding questions and registration steps.
* Translate empty states and confirmation messages.
* Translate the interactive companion’s predefined messages.
* Keep the user’s selected language across navigation and future sessions whenever persistence is available.

Do not translate only the landing page. The entire application must support all three languages.

Language implementation

Use a centralized internationalization (i18n) structure.

Store interface text in separate translation dictionaries for Kazakh, Russian, and English.

Avoid hardcoding interface text directly inside individual components.

Use consistent terminology across all pages.

Ensure that:

* Kazakh text displays correctly with all required characters.
* Russian text is grammatically natural.
* English text sounds professional and natural.
* Layouts adapt to differences in text length.
* No buttons or labels become truncated after switching languages.

The default language can be English, but users must be able to switch languages at any time.

The language selector must work in the prototype, not just appear as a decorative button.

22.4. Final Experience

The combination of a refined pastel design, the interactive companion, and multilingual support should make the platform feel distinctive and memorable.

The platform should not feel like a generic academic database.

It should feel like a welcoming digital space where students can explore opportunities, develop their interests, track their progress, and enjoy the process of building their academic future.

PART 22. FINAL QUALITY REQUIREMENTS

The final prototype must:

1. Look like a polished, professionally designed EdTech startup.
2. Have a consistent white, matcha green, and soft pink visual identity.
3. Have separate student and teacher experiences.
4. Include a complete registration and onboarding flow.
5. Have clearly separated dashboard sections.
6. Demonstrate a complete Professor Challenge workflow.
7. Demonstrate the opportunity discovery process.
8. Include a meaningful student portfolio.
9. Show how AI supports students without doing their work.
10. Include working navigation and interactive controls.
11. Use clearly labeled demo data.
12. Be responsive across devices.

Most importantly, demonstrate this complete student journey:

A student registers → chooses academic interests and subjects → discovers relevant opportunities → receives suggestions for a development plan → finds a Professor Challenge → submits an original academic assignment → receives professor feedback → adds selected achievements to their portfolio → chooses whether to make their profile visible to academic organizations.

The platform should communicate one central idea:

We don’t just help students get discovered. We help them become discoverable.